findings.body:
In GroupPermissionsV2.canManage() (no-arg, global check), the implementation is:
```
public boolean canManage() {
    if (root.hasOneAdminRole(AdminRoles.MANAGE_USERS)) return true;
    return hasPermission(null, AdminPermissionsSchema.VIEW, AdminPermissionsSchema.MANAGE);
}
```
It checks for either VIEW or MANAGE scope on the all-groups resource, instead of only MANAGE (as correctly done in the per-group overload `canManage(GroupModel group)`, which only checks `AdminPermissionsSchema.MANAGE`). As a result, an admin who was only granted the 'view' scope on the all-groups ('Groups') resource (intended for read-only access) will also satisfy `canManage()`/`requireManage()`. This method is called directly by `GroupsResource.addTopLevelGroup()` via `auth.groups().requireManage()`, so a user with only VIEW permission on groups would be able to create top-level groups - an unintended privilege escalation. The analogous Users V2 implementation (`UserPermissionsV2.canManage(user)`) correctly checks only MANAGE, confirming this is a copy/paste mistake from `canView()`.
